# Research Progress Report 

Financial Risk of Shadow AI in Healthcare: PHI Protection and Governance Decision Modeling 

Research Focus 

My research focuses on the risks that come from healthcare employees using unauthorized AI tools with sensitive clinical information. The project combines two areas that are usually looked at separately: a technical PHI protection system and a financial risk model. 

The goal is to build a system that can detect and redact sensitive patient information before it reaches an outside AI tool and then connect that performance to the financial side. I want to estimate how much Shadow AI could cost a hospital, how much risk can be reduced through better controls, and when investing in those controls actually becomes worth the cost. 

Progress So Far 

During the first three weeks, I focused on building the foundation of the project, preparing the clinical data, and setting up the financial model. 

I first collected the main real-world numbers that will be used in the model. This includes the reported 17% direct clinician use of unauthorized AI, the additional $670,000 cost linked to Shadow AI-related breaches, and the HIPAA Tier 4 penalty cap. These values give me a starting point for estimating the financial exposure created by unauthorized AI use. 

I also created three hospital types so I can compare how risk changes depending on the size of the organization: a Community Hospital, Regional Health System, and Academic Medical Center. For each hospital type, I set up different assumptions around staff size, AI use, and financial exposure. Instead of using only fixed values, I am using probability distributions so the model can reflect uncertainty and different possible situations more realistically. 

For the technical side, I processed the ASQ-PHI clinical dataset and standardized the clinical information into HIPAA Safe Harbor categories. I also kept clinical examples that contain numbers but are not actually PHI, such as lab values, vital signs, and medication dosages. This is important because I do not want the model to simply redact anything that looks sensitive and remove useful medical information. The final dataset was split into 70% training, 15% validation, and 15% testing. 

Built an Automated Clinical Data Pipeline (preprocess_clinical.py): 

I wrote a Python script to automatically load and parse clinical doctor queries from the ASQ-PHI benchmark dataset. 

I cleaned messy entity labels and standardized them into clear HIPAA categories, such as patient names, doctor names, dates, ages, locations, and ID numbers. 

I separated medical data like dosages (e.g., "50 mg") and lab results (e.g., "potassium 3.2") so the system wouldn't confuse normal clinical numbers with patient identifiers. 

I kept "hard negatives" (medical questions with zero private details) in the data so the model learns not to over-redact regular medical language. 

I formatted the text into structured prompt-and-response pairs with bracketed tags (like [PATIENT_NAME] and [DATE]). 

I automatically partitioned the processed dataset into three separate .jsonl files: 70% for training, 15% for validation, and 15% for testing. 

For the financial side, I built the first baseline Python model to calculate Annual Loss Expectancy and Return on Security Investment across the three hospital types. I also set up a bounded Poisson-based breach probability model, so the risk calculation stays realistic as the number of users and prompts increases. 

Created a Baseline Financial Calculator (static_calc.py): 

I wrote a deterministic Python script to calculate baseline Annual Loss Expectancy (ALE) and Return on Security Investment (ROSI) across small, medium, and large hospital tiers. 

I coded a bounded Poisson hazard formula ($1 - e^{-lambda) to calculate breach probability, ensuring high prompt volumes never produce impossible chances above 100%. 

I plugged in real-world benchmark numbers (a 17% clinician AI adoption rate, a $670,000 shadow AI penalty, and a $2.19M federal fine cap) to establish a baseline before running the full simulation. 

I have also started drafting the Introduction, Literature Review, and Methodology sections and have been keeping the code, data work, and documentation organized in GitHub. 

Current Stage - Week 4 

I am currently working on both the technical and financial parts of the project. 

On the technical side, I am setting up the fine-tuning process using the cleaned ASQ-PHI dataset. The main goal is to improve the model’s ability to detect PHI while still keeping the original clinical meaning of the text as much as possible. 

On the financial side, I am building the full 10,000-trial Monte Carlo simulation. Instead of producing one fixed estimate, this will allow me to generate a range of possible financial outcomes across different hospital sizes and risk levels. This should give a better picture of both normal expected losses and more severe high-cost scenarios. 

Next Steps 

The next step will be to test the fine-tuned model using precision, recall, F1-score, and inference latency. 

After that, I will connect the technical results directly to the financial model. For example, if the PHI detection system misses a certain percentage of sensitive information, that remaining exposure will affect the estimated breach probability and financial loss. 

I will then complete the Total Cost of Ownership and Return on Security Investment calculations so I can compare the cost of implementing the governance system with the amount of financial risk it is expected to reduce. The main goal will be to find the break-even point for each type of hospital. 

Overall Goal 

The final goal is to bring the technical and financial sides together into one practical framework. Instead of only showing whether a PHI protection model works, I want the research to show what that technical performance means financially for a hospital. 

Ultimately, the model should help answer: How much financial risk does Shadow AI create, how much can a PHI protection system reduce that risk, and when does investing in that system become worth it? 

 